## <a name="introduction">🤖 Introduction</a>

Welcome to the Weather App! This dynamic web application is built with Spring Boot and fetches real-time weather data using a third-party API. The app enables users to search for current weather conditions 
in any city and view detailed information such as temperature, humidity, and weather descriptions. 
Designed for both usability and learning, this project demonstrates how to integrate REST APIs, process JSON data, and create interactive web interfaces with Java and Spring Boot.


## <a name="tech-stack">⚙️ Tech Stack</a>

- Spring Boot – Provides the core backend framework for rapid application development and RESTful service integration

- Java – Main programming language powering the application logic

- Thymeleaf – Template engine for rendering dynamic HTML pages and displaying weather data

- Bootstrap/CSS: – Ensures responsive and visually appealing UI

- OpenWeatherMap API – Supplies real-time weather data in JSON format

- Jackson – Handles JSON parsing and data binding

- Lombok – Reduces boilerplate code in Java models


## <a name="features">🔋 Features</a>

👉 **City Search**: Enter any city name to instantly retrieve and display current weather conditions

👉 **Real-Time Data**: Fetches up-to-date weather information such as temperature, humidity, wind speed, and weather descriptions from the OpenWeatherMap API

👉 **Weather Icons**:  Visual representation of weather conditions using dynamic icons

👉 **Responsive Desig**: Fully responsive interface for optimal experience on all devices

👉 **Error Handling**: User-friendly messages for invalid city names or network issues


## <a name="quick-start">🤸 Quick Start</a>

Ready to run the app locally or explore the code? Here’s how you can get started:

**Prerequisites**

- Java JDK (11 or later)
- Maven or Gradle
- Git
- OpenWeatherMap API key (sign up at openweathermap.org)

**Installation**

- Clone the repository: 
   git clone <repository-url>

- Configure your API key: Add your OpenWeatherMap API key to application.properties
   weather.api.key=YOUR_API_KEY

- Install dependencies and build the project:
    mvn clean install

**Running the Project**
   mvn spring-boot:run

Open [http://localhost:8080]in your browser to view the app.

Thank you for checking out the Weather App! If you’d like to collaborate or learn more, please don’t hesitate to reach out
